            "valueString": "insufficient"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://generesolve.io/fhir/StructureDefinition/etiology-classification",
            "valueString": "multifactorial — The patient's symptoms of polyuria, polydipsia, polyphagia, and weight loss, along with elevated blood glucose and ketonuria, are classic signs of diabetes mellitus, likely type 1 given the recent onset and presence of ketones (though serum ketones were negative at follow-up). While other causes exist, the constellation strongly suggests an autoimmune process destroying pancreatic beta cells.. Diabetes mellitus type 1 is typically caused by an autoimmune destruction of pancreatic beta cells leading to insulin deficiency. This is considered a multifactorial disease involving genetic predisposition (multiple genes) and environmental triggers (like viral infections) interacting to initiate the autoimmune response. It is not primarily caused by a single gene defect, a chromosomal abnormality, or a non-genetic acquired cause"
          },
          {
            "url": "https://generesolve.io/fhir/StructureDefinition/sources-consulted",
            "valueString": "hpo_a, open_targets, gwas_catalog, europepmc"
          },
          {
